    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im having a bit of a headache with aws. i'm trying to get a lambda function to write some logs to an s3 bucket but i keep getting an access denied error. i thought i set the iam policy right but it just wont work. can u take a look at this json and see what i missed?\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": \"s3:PutObject\",\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your IAM policy looks like it's missing the necessary permissions for the Lambda function to actually *be* allowed to interact with S3. It's a common issue, and you might be missing a dependency, like a specific service role or perhaps the bucket policy itself is rejecting the request. Try double-checking if the Lambda's execution role has been correctly attached and if there are any explicit Deny statements in the bucket policy that might be blocking the write operation. Sometimes, the error isn't in the policy you're looking at, but in the surrounding infrastructure setup.",
      "source": "target",
